{"language":"FCL-1","records":[{"id":"r1","type":"claim","text":"The dispute pattern is re-poseable as over-coupling: three people with unstable schedules hold more shared dependencies than their current coordination capacity supports, so shrinking the shared surface is a rival remedy to improving agreement mechanics.","grounds":"Recurring arguments, schedule churn, and one flatmate's withdrawal are all consistent with coordination load exceeding capacity, not only with underspecified agreements."},{"id":"r2","type":"claim","text":"'We agree, then disagree about what it meant' is also the signature of divergent standards (different thresholds for what counts as done); explicitness does not resolve divergent standards and can relocate or harden the fight when one side's threshold gets written in."},{"id":"r3","type":"objection","text":"The selected account's remedy imports workplace apparatus (written lists, definitions of done, claiming, review dates) into a friendship; under low trust, written tracking reads as exactly the silent score-keeping that account itself rules out.","bearing":"If the irritant is ownership or trust rather than information loss, formalization raises the cost it aims to remove. The bearing is itself criticizable: some flats accept lightweight structure without reading it as surveillance."},{"id":"r4","type":"claim","text":"The avoiding friend's withdrawal may be a verdict on the user-convened process specifically rather than on chores or conversation in general; if so, the effective move is the convener standing down rather than a better-designed system from the same convener.","scope":"Conjecture; consistent with, but not established by, the exposed description."},{"id":"r5","type":"commitment","text":"Before proposing any system, the user tells both flatmates they are done managing the chore question, then asks each separately what outcome they want, explicitly including decoupling options: separate shelves and cooking, domain ownership with swappable authority over each domain's standard, cost-split paid cleaning, or a time-limited agreement to tolerate more mess.","scope":"Applies where the user has been the de facto convener and designer of the process.","depends":["r1","r4"]},{"id":"r6","type":"problem","text":"What evidence discriminates between underspecification and standards-or-trust explanations? Proposed cheap discriminators: whether disputes recur over tasks that were written down, and whether the avoider re-engages under a different convener or after the user stops proposing."},{"id":"r7","type":"use","text":"Treat the two framings as complementary in sequence: decouple what is cheap to decouple first, then apply explicitness tools to whatever genuinely shared remainder survives; the discriminating checks guide how far to go in each direction.","depends":["r1","r2","r6"]}],"uptake":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r6","r7"]}